          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong absolute colimit
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserved
            by any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            whatsoever. In general this happens because the colimit is a colimit for purely “diagrammatic” reasons. The notion is most important in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
          %p
            Of course, there is a dual notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong absolute limit
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p The term “absolute colimit” is actually used for two closely related, but distinct, notions.
          %h3#particular_absolute_colimits Particular absolute colimits
          #DefParticular.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A particular
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              diagram in a particular
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is an
              %strong absolute colimit
              if it is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserved
              by every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              with domain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            This definition makes sense also in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+colimit"} weighted colimit
            in a particular
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is an
            %strong absolute colimit
            if it is preserved by every
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Note, however, that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+colimit"} conical colimit
            in a
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            may be preserved by all
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi o
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_o
          %p
            Generalizing in a different direction, absolute colimits in
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                postulated colimit
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/postulated+colimit"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sites"} sites
            where the site has the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+topology"} trivial topology
          %h3#weights_for_absolute_colimits Weights for absolute colimits
          #DefWeight.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For a given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+colimit"} weight
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi\colon D^{op} \to V
              for colimits is an
              = succeed "," do
                %strong absolute weight
              = succeed "," do
                %strong weight for absolute colimits
              = succeed "-weighted" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
              %em all
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "-functors." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Absolute colimits of this sort are also called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Cauchy colimits
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            which admits all absolute colimits — that is, all
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete
            %em limits
            weighted by all weights for absolute limits.
          %h2#characterizations Characterizations
          %p Both types of absolute colimits admit pleasant characterizations.
          %h3#particular_absolute_colimits_2 Particular absolute colimits
          %p
            For a particular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocone"} cocone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \colon F \to \Delta A
            under a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon I\to C
            (all in the Set-enriched world), the following are equivalent:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                is an absolute colimiting cocone.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                is a colimiting cocone which is is preserved by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo ↪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Set
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \hookrightarrow [C^{op},Set]
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                is a colimiting cocone which is preserved by the representable functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(F(i),-)\colon C\to Set
                (for all
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(A,-)\colon C\to Set
            %li
              %p
                There exists
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0\in I
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0\colon A\to F(i_0)
                such that
              %ol
                %li
                  For every
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0 \circ \mu_i
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{F(i)}
                  are in the same connected component of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F(i) / F)
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi i
                              %mn 0
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_{i_0} \circ d_0 = 1_{A}
                  \.
          %p
            The equivalence of the first two is basically because the Yoneda embedding is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            (as is explained in more detail in the special case of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+coequalizers"} absolute coequalizers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is colimiting, and it is clearly a property preserved by any functor.
          %p
            Note that in particular, the fourth condition implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(i_0)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+colimits"} weak colimits
          %p
            It is also possible to prove directly that the third condition implies the first two, without extracting the fourth condition along the way. Namely, Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            be the full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consisting of the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(i)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            defines a functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\to B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is also the colimit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            %em absolute
            colimit of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\hookrightarrow C
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an absolute colimit of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %h3#CharacterizationsWeightsForAbsoluteColimits Weights for absolute colimits
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a Bénabou
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmos"} cosmos
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi K
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J\colon K &amp;#x21F8; A
            a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                is a weight for absolute colimits (i.e.
                = succeed "-weighted" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi J
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "-functors)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^*
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
            %li
              %p
                There is a weight
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mi ⇸
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^*\colon A &amp;#x21F8; K
                such that
                = succeed "-weighted" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi J
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                = succeed "-weighted" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi J
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^*
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#particular_absolute_colimits_3 Particular absolute colimits
          %p
            Of course, every colimit weighted by a weight for absolute colimits is itself a particular absolute colimit. But it may also happen that a particular colimit may be absolute without all colimits of that shape being absolute. For example (in ordinary category theory, with
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V=Set
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+coequalizers"} split coequalizers
              are absolute, and figure in Beck’s
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            %li
              More generally (of course),
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+coequalizers"} absolute coequalizers
              are absolute.
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+pushouts"} absolute pushouts
              appear in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elegant+Reedy+categories"} elegant Reedy categories
          %p We can also say something about non-examples.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Initial objects (in
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                %em never
                absolute. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                is an initial object, then it is never preserved by the representable functor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(0,-)\colon C \to Set
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, coproducts in
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h3#WExampleseightsForAbsoluteColimits Weights for absolute colimits
          %p
            In ordinary
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+idempotents"}> split idempotents
              \.
          %p
            In fact, this example is “universal,” in that an ordinary category is Cauchy complete iff it has split idempotents, although not every absolute colimit “is” the splitting of an idempotent. More precisely, the class of absolute
            = succeed "-limits" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/saturated+class+of+limits"} saturation
            of idempotent-splittings.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            there can be more types of absolute colimits. For instance:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in categories with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphisms"} zero morphisms
                (that is, enriched over
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+objects"} initial objects
                are absolute.
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+categories"} Ab-enriched categories
                (or, more generally, categories enriched over commutative
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
                are absolute. Finite biproducts and splitting of idempotents together are “universal” absolute colimits for Ab-enrichment.
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SupLat"} SupLat
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-categories"} dg-categories
                (or more generally, categories enriched over
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+set"} graded sets
                %em shifts/suspensions
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cones"} mapping cones
                are absolute.
            %li
              %p
                in Lawvere
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            %li
              %p
                in categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory-enriched+category"} enriched
                over the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
                (or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                %em gluings
                are absolute. In this case the enriched categories can roughly be identified with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaves"} separated presheaves
                and the Cauchy-complete ones with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            %li
              %p
                in categories enriched over
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+actions"} group actions
                are absolute.
          %p
            New kinds of absolute (co)limits also arise in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
                (enriched over
                = succeed "-groupoids)," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
                (which are enriched, in the
                = succeed "-categorical" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushouts"} pushouts
                are absolute, and therefore so are all finite colimits.
          %h2#generalisations Generalisations
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K : A \to C
            is a functor, then a colimit in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              \-absolute
            if it is preserved by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi K
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_K : C \to [A^{\mathrm{op}}, \mathscr{V}]
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            to be the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Absolute colimits in enriched categories
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1983__24_4_377_0/"} Cahiers 1983
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Pare"}> Robert Pare
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On absolute colimits
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Basic Concepts of Enriched Category Theory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ‘s answer to
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/a/267324"}
                %em
                  What are
                  %em all
                  of the exactness properties enjoyed by stable ∞-categories?
          %p A generalisation of Street’s results about left adjoints and absolute weights is proven in Proposition 6.2 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"} John Power
              = succeed "," do
                %em An enriched view on the extended finitary monad–Lawvere theory correspondence
        .revisedby
